STEP 1 -- baseline with NO filter: both management interfaces answer             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: send a known count of frames from each host NIC while the DUT carries no
  access list.  This proves the instrument before any silence later in the case is read
  as a filter.  Data inside a TCP SYN is legal but unusual.  If the DUT had ignored these
  SYNs, every later silence would have looked like a filter when it was really a dead
  instrument.

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag base-eth3 --out /tmp/mgmt1159/T33386 --iface eth3 \
       --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:23 --icmp 10.38.22.34 \
       --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e \
       --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
  cap base-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :23    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :23    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]
    x1     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.34     tell 10.38.22.33
    x1     ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34     is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag base-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 \
       --icmp 10.38.22.66 --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f \
       --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4
  cap base-eth4-eth4.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.66    :22    -> 10.38.22.65    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    R
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.66     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]

  <-- 4 of 4 SYNs to TCP/22 were answered `SA` on BOTH management addresses, so both
      are open.
  <-- 4 of 4 SYNs to TCP/23 were answered `RA`.  The telnet server is off.  This flow is
      here on purpose: it shows what a REFUSED service looks like, so a FILTERED frame
      later cannot be confused with it.
  <-- 4 of 4 ICMP echoes were answered on both addresses.
  <-- The `R` rows come from the testbox kernel.  Its own stack never opened these
      connections, so it resets the SYN-ACK.  That is expected with a crafted SYN and it
      does not change any count above.
  => STEP 1 PASS: with no filter, both management interfaces answer on TCP/22 and to
     ICMP, and the instrument is proven.  Every number later in this case is compared
     against these.

STEP 2 -- a NUMBERED hardware access list keeps only its last rule, silently      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a management filter is "permit the named host, then deny the rest".  That needs
  at least two rules.  The three rules below were sent through `tools/cfg.py`, which
  stops at the first `% ` line the device prints.

awplus(config)#access-list 3010 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
awplus(config)#access-list 3010 deny tcp any 10.38.22.32/27 eq 22
awplus(config)#access-list 3010 deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group 3010
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#access-group 3010

  <-- Every command was accepted.  The device printed no `% ` line and the tool exited 0.

  What the device actually holds afterwards:

awplus#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3010
      4 deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22
awplus#show running-config | include access-list
  access-list 3010 deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22

  <-- Only the LAST of the three rules is there.  The permit rule and the first deny rule
      are gone.  There was no error, no warning, and nothing in the running-config to
      show that two rules were discarded.  A numbered hardware access list on this
      platform holds exactly one rule: each `access-list <n> <rule>` REPLACES the one
      before it.

  The DUT's own log agrees, and the line arrived while these three rules were being
  entered:

  2026 Oct 21 01:00:18 local7.err awplus EXFX[1015]: DBG:exfx_cExt_dxch_rules_pclIdActionUpdate 7076: Didn't find matching rule (to id 1) for the copying from one (from id 0)

  <-- The hardware access-list driver logged an error about a second rule slot that was
      not there, at the moment the second rule should have been installed.

  The one rule that survived denies TCP/22 to 10.38.22.64/27, which is vlan48
  10.38.22.66.  vlan10 10.38.22.34 is in 10.38.22.32/27, which this rule does not match.
  A hardware access list PERMITS what no rule denies, so vlan10 stays open.

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag num-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 \
       --icmp 10.38.22.66 ...
  cap num-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.66     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag num-eth3 ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22 \
       --icmp 10.38.22.34 ...
  cap num-eth3-eth3.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]

  flow                                        sent     answered   reading
  eth4 -> 10.38.22.66:22, the denied address  4 SYN    0          dropped in hardware.
                                                                  The same 4 frames got
                                                                  4 `SA` in STEP 1
  eth4 -> 10.38.22.66 ICMP echo (control)     4        4 replies  the port, the link and
                                                                  the SVI are alive, so
                                                                  the deny is selective
  eth3 -> 10.38.22.34:22 (control, other      4 SYN    4 `SA`     not matched by the
  destination subnet)                                             rule, so still open

awplus#show access-list counters
  Name: 3010
  	Rule: deny tcp any 10.38.22.64/27 eq 22
  	Packet Hits: 4

  <-- The counter read 0 before the leg and 4 after it: the four SYNs.  The device's own
      count and the captured frames agree.
  => STEP 2 PASS: a filter does deny management traffic in the dataplane, and the count
     on the device matches the wire.  The step also found a defect.  A numbered hardware
     access list silently keeps only the last rule entered.  A management filter is
     always permit-then-deny, so written in the numbered form it collapses to the deny
     alone, and that locks out the administrator's own host.  The only warning the
     operator gets is a `show access-list` they may never run.

STEP 3 -- a NAMED hardware access list, permit then deny: the TPS 6.1.1 filter    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the named sub-mode is the form that holds more than one rule.  The numbered
  list of STEP 2 was unbound and deleted first, so the same two-rule intent is entered
  twice in two different forms.  That is a direct A/B comparison.

awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#no access-group 3010
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#no access-group 3010
awplus(config)#no access-list 3010
awplus(config)#access-list hardware ckmgmt
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#exit
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt

awplus#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list ckmgmt
      4 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
      8 deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus#show running-config | include access
  access-list hardware ckmgmt
   permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
   deny tcp any any eq 22
   access-group ckmgmt        [under port1.0.10]
   access-group ckmgmt        [under port1.0.48]

  <-- Both rules are there, in the order they were entered.  The same two-rule intent was
      cut down to one rule in the numbered form in STEP 2.
  <-- This list is exactly the sentence of TPS 6.1.1: permit the host the operator names,
      deny everything else that is addressed to the management interface on that service.

