  => STEP 3 PASS: the first statement of TPS 6.1.1 is satisfied.  The host the operator
     named reaches the management service and the host it did not name is denied, under
     one configuration, on the same hardware, with two controls in the same captures.

STEP 4 -- removal: the filter leaves the configuration AND the dataplane          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the step text is the floor.  Removal is proved as well, on the wire.

awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#no access-list hardware ckmgmt

awplus#show access-list
                                              <-- empty, no list at all
awplus#show running-config | include access
   switchport mode access ...                 <-- the only `access` lines left belong to
                                                  switchport; no access-list, no
                                                  access-group

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag removed-eth4 ... --iface eth4 --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 ... \
       --count 4
  cap removed-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.66    :22    -> 10.38.22.65    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.65    :40000 -> 10.38.22.66    :22    R

  => STEP 4 PASS: the host that was denied is answered 4 of 4 `SA` again.  The filter is
     gone from the configuration and from the dataplane, not only from the configuration.

STEP 5 -- a second filter mechanism: an access-list number on an SNMP community   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: TPS 6.1.1 names SNMP.  This mechanism uses a STANDARD access list, and a
  standard list DENIES what it does not permit.  That is the opposite default to the
  hardware list of STEP 3, so the reading of this step is the other way round.

awplus(config)#access-list 10 permit 10.38.22.33 0.0.0.0
awplus(config)#snmp-server
awplus(config)#snmp-server community ckacl ro 10        <- the filtered community
awplus(config)#snmp-server community ckopen ro          <- the CONTROL community, no ACL

  <-- `snmp-server` was already in the running-config before this case, so that line
      changed nothing and it is not removed at the end.

awplus#show access-list
  Standard IP access list 10
      4 permit 10.38.22.33
awplus#show running-config | include snmp-server
  snmp-server
  snmp-server community ckacl 10
  snmp-server community ckopen

  <-- `ro` is the default and the device does not store it.  `snmp-server community ckacl
      ro 10` reads back as `snmp-server community ckacl 10`.

  The 2x2 matrix, run with `tools/snmpq.py`, which names the outcome instead of returning
  a bare exit code:
    snmpq.py get <agent> 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 --v2c --community <c> --timeout 3 --retries 1
  The route decides the source address.  A query to 10.38.22.34 leaves from eth3
  (10.38.22.33).  A query to 10.38.22.66 leaves from eth4 (10.38.22.65).

  source                       community          result
  10.38.22.33 (permitted)      ckacl (filtered)   RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus
  10.38.22.65 (not permitted)  ckacl (filtered)   RESULT TIMEOUT -- Timeout: No Response from 10.38.22.66.
  10.38.22.65 (not permitted)  ckopen (control)   RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus
  10.38.22.33                  ckopen             RESULT VALUE -- .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus

  <-- Exactly one cell of four fails, and it is the cell the filter names.  The control
      community answered the SAME source host over the SAME path in the same minute.  So
      the timeout is the access list.  It is not a lost packet, not a dead agent and not
      an unreachable address.

  The DUT logged the denial itself, at the moment of the denied query:

  2026 Oct 21 01:05:30 kern.err awplus snmpd[1818]: Received SNMP Packet from illegal server 10.38.22.65
  2026 Oct 21 01:05:53 kern.err awplus snmpd[525]: Last message 'Received SNMP Packet' repeated 1 times, suppressed by syslog-ng on awplus

  => STEP 5 PASS: a second, independent mechanism filters management access by source
     host, and TPS 6.1.1's named example (SNMP) is covered.  The two mechanisms work in
     different places.  The hardware access list of STEP 3 drops the frame in silicon and
     the DUT never sees it.  The SNMP community access list is enforced inside the agent:
     the packet arrives, is checked, is discarded, and a log line is written.  To the
     client both look like the same silence.

STEP 6 -- the second statement of TPS 6.1.1: the service port is configurable     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config)#ssh server 2222
awplus#show ssh server
  SSH Server                         : Enabled
  Protocol                           : IPv4,IPv6
  Port                               : 2222
awplus#show running-config | include ssh
  ssh server 2222
  ssh server allow-users manager
  service ssh

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag port2222 ... --iface eth3 \
       --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:2222 ... --count 4
  cap port2222-eth3.pcap: 20 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA   <-- nothing listens
                                                                        on 22 now
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :2222  S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :2222  -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA   <-- the service is
                                                                        here now
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :2222  R

  <-- Both ports were probed in ONE leg, 4 frames each.  TCP/22 went from `SA` in STEP 1
      to `RA`, and TCP/2222 went from no listener to `SA`.  The service really moved.

  Put back, and the restore proved on the wire as well:

awplus(config)#ssh server 22
awplus#show running-config | include ssh
  ssh server allow-users manager
  service ssh                                 <-- the explicit port line is GONE: 22 is
                                                  the default and is not stored
tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag port-restored ... --iface eth3 \
       --tcp 10.38.22.34:22,10.38.22.34:2222 ... --count 4
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA   <-- back
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :2222  -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 RA   <-- gone

  => STEP 6 PASS: the second statement of TPS 6.1.1 is satisfied.  The TCP port number of
     a management service can be changed, and the change is real on the wire, not only in
     a `show` command.

STEP 7 -- the filter survives a restart, with nothing typed after the boot        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: put the named filter of STEP 3 back, save it, reload the DUT, then measure both
  halves again before anyone logs in.  The DUT started this step with no access list of
  any kind, which STEP 4 left behind.

  Pre-check with no filter, so the instrument is proved again first:

tb277# sudo arpleg.py --tag t386-base-eth4 --out /tmp/ntp1021/T33386 --iface eth4 \
       --tcp 10.38.22.66:22 --icmp 10.38.22.66 --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 \
       --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
  cap t386-base-eth4-eth4.pcap: 24 frame(s)
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.66:22    -> 10.38.22.65:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   R
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65      [ours]

  The filter, in the named form:

awplus(config)#access-list hardware ckmgmt
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckmgmt
awplus#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list ckmgmt
      4 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
      8 deny tcp any any eq 22

  cap t386-acl-eth4-eth4.pcap: 12 frame(s)                <- the DENIED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65      [ours]
  cap t386-acl-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)                <- the PERMITTED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]

  <-- Both halves work before the restart.  Denied host: 4 SYNs, no reply, ICMP 4 of 4.
      Permitted host: 4 `SA`, with `:23` still answering `RA`.

  Saved, then reloaded:

awplus#write
  Building configuration...
  [OK]
awplus#show startup-config | include access-list|access-group|permit|deny
  access-list hardware ckmgmt
   permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
   deny tcp any any eq 22
   access-group ckmgmt
   access-group ckmgmt

tb277# tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
  >>> reload   (04:46:05 UTC)
  reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
  login seen after 60 s

  The legs were settled before they were read.  The first ping on eth3 after the boot
  returned 0 of 4, 100 percent packet loss.  A second ping seconds later returned 4 of 4,
  and the box's neighbour table then read
  `10.38.22.34 dev eth3 lladdr e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 REACHABLE`.  eth4 was 4 of 4 at once.
  Read at the first attempt, eth3's missing TCP replies would have looked like the filter
  blocking the host it is supposed to PERMIT, which is the opposite of the truth.

  The real legs, 36 s after the login prompt, with NOTHING typed into the DUT:

  cap t386-afterboot-eth4-eth4.pcap: 14 frame(s)          <- the DENIED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65      [ours]
    x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.65 tell 10.38.22.66
    x1   ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.65 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f
  cap t386-afterboot-eth3-eth3.pcap: 30 frame(s)          <- the PERMITTED host
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:22    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.33:40000 -> 10.38.22.34:23   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.34:23    -> 10.38.22.33:40000 RA
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34      [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33      [ours]

  <-- Both halves survived the restart.  The deny still drops the host that is not named,
      silently, while that host's ICMP is answered 4 of 4.  The permit still lets the
      named host complete a handshake.  The `RA` control still separates a refused
      service from a filtered frame.

  Only after the legs was the console opened:

awplus#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list ckmgmt
      4 permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
      8 deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus#show running-config | include access-group
  access-group ckmgmt
   access-group ckmgmt
awplus#show access-list counters
  Hardware ACL Packet Counters
  Name: ckmgmt
  	Rule: permit tcp 10.38.22.33/32 any eq 22
  	Packet Hits: 8
  	Rule: deny tcp any any eq 22
  	Packet Hits: 4

  <-- The reboot set the counters back to zero, and after it they account for the
      post-boot legs exactly: deny 4 is eth4's four SYNs, permit 8 is eth3's four SYNs
      plus its four RSTs.  Nothing is left over from before the reload.

  Removed again, and the removal proved on the wire:

awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt          [port1.0.10]
awplus(config-if)#no access-group ckmgmt          [port1.0.48]
awplus(config)#no access-list hardware ckmgmt
awplus#show access-list
     (nothing)
  cap t386-removed-eth4-eth4.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   S   [ours]
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.66:22    -> 10.38.22.65:40000 SA
    x4   TCP  10.38.22.65:40000 -> 10.38.22.66:22   R
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 ... [ours] ; x4 ICMP t0/c0 ... [ours]

  => STEP 7 PASS: a management access list bound with `access-group` is saved and
     restored like any other configuration.  After a 60 s cold boot, with nothing typed
     into the device, the host that is not named still got 0 replies to 4 SYNs while its
     ICMP was answered 4 of 4, and the named host still completed its handshake with 4
     `SA`.  The arc on the wire across this step is: open -> dropped -> (cold boot) ->
     dropped -> open.  So TPS 6.1.1 holds across a restart, not only inside one session.
